# The annotations of the game image $60C0-$FDFF (tape block 5, loaded by the stub at $5B00): the single source of
# truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py --case .). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
#
# Witness: "L1 N" is the number of runs of an entry's or a label's instruction summed over the 70 builds of case.json
# (c1, power-on with the tape and keyboard play, and the 69 builds of work/stage1/rec: windows of the 80 000-frame RZX
# walkthrough, the free-play windows, energy-out, game-over, the menu choices, room-23, room-tour and win), from
# work/stage2/code.lst (work/stage2/lst.py). "L1 -" or "known only from the code" marks what no build ran.
# Coordinates: the droid's x is a character column 0-$1D, its y a pixel row in steps of 2 ($30-$98); the play area is
# the screen's rows 6-23 ($40C0-$57FF, attributes $58C0-$5AFF).

@equ $02BF KEYBOARD ; the ROM's keyboard scan (LAST_K, FRAMES), called by the interrupt handler
@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the display file
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the screen attributes
@equ $5B5B ImJump 3 ; the JP Interrupt the IM 2 table's vector $5B5B jumps through (SetupIM2 writes it)
@equ $FE00 Im2Table 257 ; the IM 2 vector table: 257 bytes of $5B (SetupIM2), so every vector is $5B5B
@equ $FF01 SpriteList 255 ; the sprite list: 8-byte records (graphic, flag pointer, width, rows, screen address), a zero word ends it

@isub $6CC9 LD HL,TileGraphics-2
@isub $6E65 LD HL,WeaponColours-1
@isub $90D8 LD HL,EnemyFrameCounts-1
@isub $90E2 LD HL,EnemyGraphics-2
@isub $7B54 LD HL,Font-256
@isub $7B7A LD HL,Font-256
@isub $8AB9 LD BC,Font-256
@isub $9699 LD DE,Font-257
@isub $6E34 LD HL,EdPieces-352
@isub $6CBD LD HL,TileAttrs-2

@module cells ; The cell table: one byte for each character cell of the play area
@about The cell table mirrors the attribute file: the cell of screen character (row, column) is $6000 + 32 x row + column, so its attribute address is the cell address with H XOR $38. Rows 0-5 ($6000-$60BF) lie under the BASIC program and are never play cells; the image holds rows 6-23 ($60C0-$62FF). DrawRoom $6B22 rebuilds it for every room (1 where the attribute is not 0: a wall), then puts the room's items and teleporter in; the movers mark the cells they stand on (bit 1 an enemy, bit 2 an object, bit 3 a patroller) and the shots the cells they pass (bits 4-6).

$60C0 b CellTable 32 ; The cell table, rows 6-23 of the screen: 0 empty, 1 a wall, $80+ an item, $FF $FF a teleporter, bits 1-6 the movers' marks
> Values: 0 empty; 1 a wall (DrawRoom $6D8C, where the room's attribute is not 0); an item's code on its 2 x 2 cells (DrawItem $6E0D: $80 GEN 19, $81 an energy source, $C1-$C3 weapons 1-3, $82-$B3 the ED pieces); $FF $FF on the two top cells of a teleporter (DrawRoom $6DE3). Marks a mover XORs in and out: bit 1 an enemy (MoveEnemy $90E7 through XorCells $7D87 with 2), bit 2 an object (4), bit 3 a patroller (8); the shots OR bits 4-6 (OrCells $7DAE: $11, $21 and $41 for weapons 3, 2 and 1), which the objects test against their kind (ObjectKind $7C28).
> Readers: CellAddr $7CAA (the droid's cell, the step tests), CellAddrXY $7C94, the touch test of MoveDroid ($75C8), MoveEnemy's spawn ($911B). The bytes in the image are the last room of the authors' session (no build reads them before DrawRoom writes the table: c1 frame 13580 calls DrawRoom first).

@module start ; The entry point and the work space
@about The game block is entered at $6300 (the stub's JP $6300). The work space that follows holds the movers' records, the droid, the shot, the room and the scroller, then the scroller's messages and the buffers. Many of its bytes in the image are the authors' assembler source (Leftover text, src/res/leftover): the game writes those variables before it reads them.

$6300 c Start ; The game's entry: JP Setup
> The stub at $5B00 jumps here after the load (c1: L1 1).

$6303 b Enemies 9 ; The four enemy records of 9 bytes
> Record k (k = 0-3) at $6303 + 9k: +0 the timer (MoveEnemy calls to the next act), +1 the state (bits 0-1 the diagonal direction: 0 right and down, 1 left and down, 2 left and up, 3 right and up; bit 6 materialising; bit 7 hit, the explosion; $40 with +0 = 1 or 2 a spawn waiting), +2 x in pixels (up to $F0; a spawn writes Random OR $20 AND $F8, $20-$D0), +3 y in pixels (up to $B0), +4/+5 the graphic pointer, +6 the frame, +7 the frames, +8 the attribute. DrawRoom $6B40 zeroes byte 0 of each and, by the bits of the room's enemy mask, sets +0 to 1 or 2, +1 to $40 and the graphic to BlankGraphic $6609. MainLoop calls MoveEnemy $90E7 with each record (L1 18 579 passes).
$630C Enemy2
$6315 Enemy3
$631E Enemy4

$6327 b Objects 10 ; The room's two object records of 10 bytes
> Object 1 at $6327, object 2 at $6331: +0 the kind (bits 4-7, ObjectKind) and a 2-pass move timer (bits 0-1; 0: no object), +1 the state (bits 0-1 the direction, bit 4 dead (MoveObject returns at once), bit 5 stopped by another weapon (cleared again when its own weapon kills it: MoveObjectDying RES 5), bit 6 just entered, bit 7 dying), +2 x (pixels), +3 y, +4/+5 the graphic, +6 the frame, +7 the frames, +8 the weapon mark it dies to, +9 the attribute. DrawRoom copies the room's records from the alien map into these (LDIR at $6C2B), SaveRoomObjects $6ADC writes them back when the droid leaves the room, so an object keeps its place and its life. MainLoop calls MoveObject $809D for each (L1 18 579).
$6328 Object1Flags
$6331 Object2
$6332 Object2Flags

$633B b Patrollers 8 ; The three patroller records of 8 bytes, and their pointers
> A patroller moves along a fixed row through several rooms: +0 bit 7 not drawn (DrawRoom sets it on every room change), the frame; +1 y and the direction; +2 the column; +3 the room it is in; +4/+5 the right end, the room and column where it turns left; +6/+7 the left end, where it turns right (the end column itself is stepped back at once). MovePatrollers $6AAD takes one a pass through PatrollerPtrs. The bytes in the image are the start state (no NewGame resets them).
$6343 Patroller2
$634B Patroller3
$6353 PatrollerPtrs ; the addresses of the three records, read by MovePatrollers

$6359 b DroidVars 8 ; The droid, the shot, the room and the energy counters
> x (column 0-$1D), y (pixel row $30-$98 in steps of 2), the flags (bit 0 facing left, bit 1 turning, bit 2 in the air (no wall under the feet), bit 3 moving, bit 4 the shield (down held alone: FlagPointer $0000, EnergyReload 10, HazardFlash), bit 6 the step frame, bit 7 the sprite list's collision bit), the frame 0-13, the width in bytes. The image's bytes from $6359 are Leftover text (the authors' source): PlaceDroid $7A6D and NewGame $7A2F write every byte before play.
@res leftover/Source1.bin
$635A DroidY
$635B DroidFlags ; bit 0 left, 1 turning round, 2 in the air, 3 moving, 4 the shield (DOWN alone while standing), 6 the walk frame flag, 7 collided (the interrupt's XOR found ink)
$635C DroidFrame ; the frame 0-13 (DroidFrames $6888)
$635D DroidWidth ; the frame's width in bytes (DroidWidths $68A4)
$635E Shot ; the shot: 0 none, else the weapon's state; then x, y and the graphic (Fire $861C, MoveShot $8298)
$635F ShotPos
$6361 ShotGraphic
$6363 Room ; the room 0-$7F the droid is in ($54 at the start: NewGame $7A51)
$6364 ControlsLatch ; the controls as MoveDroid last took them
$6366 EnergyTimer ; interrupts to the next energy step (EnergyTick $7AB6)
$6367 EnemyKindTurn ; the enemy kind cycle (EnemyKind $90C8)
$6368 Weapons ; the weapon in use: bit 7 weapon 0 (unlimited), bits 6, 5, 4 weapons 1-3
$6369 KeyLatches ; bit 0 SPACE released, bit 1 ENTER released, bit 6 set: fire must be released before the next shot, bit 7 no fire
$636A Ammo ; ammunition of weapons 1-3 (BCD)
$636D Lives ; lives (BCD, 3 at the start)
$636E Energy ; energy (BCD, 99 at the start and after a life)
$636F MoveDelay ; the passes to the droid's next step
$6370 DeathColour ; the colour step of the death flash (HazardFlash $699B)
$6373 PatrollerTurn ; the patroller MovePatrollers moves next 0-2
$6374 EnergyReload ; the energy timer's reload: $FA, $0A under the shield or after an enemy's touch
$6375 BorderSound ; the last value sent to port $FE (the beeper bit toggles)
$6376 TeleportLatch ; 1 while the droid stands on a teleporter it came through
$6377 TouchTimer ; interrupts the fast drain lasts (50 after a touch, DrainTimer $7906)
$6378 DroidGraphic ; the droid's frame graphic (DroidSave copies it every pass)
$637A TurnGraphic ; the frame the turn-round mirrors (TurnRound $7866)
$637C TurnStep ; the pointer into TurnSequence $68B2
$637E FlagPointer ; the byte the interrupt sets bit 7 of when the droid's sprite meets ink

$6380 b InitialVars 8 ; The start values of $6364-$637F, copied by NewGame
> NewGame $7A59 copies these 28 bytes to ControlsLatch (L1 4 new games, LDIR 112 runs). Energy timer $FA, weapon 0, ammunition 3 3 3, lives 3, energy 99, move delay 1, the drain reload $FA, DroidGraphic BlankGraphic, TurnGraphic DroidFrames' first ($9C40), TurnStep TurnSequence, FlagPointer DroidFlags.

$639C b RoomVars 8 ; The room's items, teleporter and the scratch words
> From the room's alien record (DrawRoom $6B6C): the two items' positions and kinds, the teleporter's position and target room; then the random number, the patroller pointer, the item cell found and scratch words of the movers and the shots. The image's bytes are Leftover text (the authors' source).
@res leftover/Source2.bin
$639D Item2Pos
$639E Item1Kind
$639F Item2Kind
$63A0 TeleportPos ; the teleporter's position byte (0: none)
$63A1 TeleportRoom ; the room the teleporter sends to
$63A2 TeleportColumn
$63A3 TeleportRow
$63A4 RandomSeed ; four bytes, Random $7DC9
$63A8 PatrollerPtr ; the record MovePatrollers works on
$63AA FoundItem ; the cell of an item the droid stands on (0: none)
$63AE MoverScratch ; the interrupt's width; the exhaust's pointers
$63B1 DrawMode ; DrawRoom's A: 0 a scroll, else a clear; the item found
$63B2 RoomRecordPtr ; DrawRoom: the alien record pointer while it fills the enemy records
$63B4 RoomEnemyPtr ; DrawRoom: the enemy record in hand
$63B6 TileAttrPtr ; DrawRoom: the attribute data of the tile in hand
$63B8 ObjectScratch
$63BC StepX ; the step routines' x, y, width, height
$63BD StepY
$63BE StepWidth
$63BF StepHeight
$63C0 ShotScratch
$63C1 ShotMark ; the cell bits the shot ORs in
$63C2 Controls ; bits 0-4 right, left, down, up, fire; bit 5 paused; bits 7-6 the method ($80 keyboard, $40 cursor, 0 Kempston)
$63C3 TouchBits ; the OR of the cells under the droid (MoveDroid $75C3)
$63C4 ObjectBits ; MoveObject: the OR of the cells under an object
$63C5 BeamScreen ; the teleport beam's screen address (Teleport $7719)
$63C7 ScrollText ; the message the scroller is printing (0: none)
$63C9 ScrollNext ; the message waiting
$63CB ScrollPriority ; the priority of the message shown or waiting
$63CC ScrollLine ; the pixel line 0-7 of the text row the scroller brings in
$63CD ScrollDelay ; interrupts to the next scroll step (5, 15 at a character)
$63CE ScrollScreen ; the screen address the scroller prints to
$63D0 SpriteListEnd ; the end of the sprite list (SpriteList $FF01)

@module messages ; The scroller's messages
@about A message is a priority byte and its text, upper case, ending in '.'. QueueMessage $6ACD takes a message only when its priority is above the one shown (ScrollPriority); the interrupt's scroller $9640 scrolls it up a pixel line at a time, 19 characters a row, through the window at $402C (so the texts are padded to rows of 19). The editable form is src/res/text/Messages.txt (one message a line: the priority, a tab, the text).

$63D2 b Messages 8 ; The ten messages: a priority byte, the text, '.'
> Readers: QueueMessage $6ACD (the priority), the interrupt's scroller $96A0 (the text: L1 reads $63D2-$65AD). Queued by: Restart $690A (TUJAD FROM ORPHEUS), WinTest $69F3 (EDS COMPLETED), EnergyTick $7AC9 (WARNING ENERGY LOW), the pickups $7687 / $766A / $7671 (ENERGY SOURCE ABSORBED, WEAPON LOCATED AND STORED, GEN 19 COLLECTED), Teleport $778B (TRANSPORTING), LoseLife $6A51 (GEN 19 DESTROYED), the win $6A10 (CONGRATULATIONS) and the game over $6A94 (COMPUTER MALFUNCTION).
@res text/Messages.bin
$63E7 MsgEdsCompleted
$6422 MsgEnergyLow
$6437 MsgEnergyAbsorbed
$645F MsgTransporting
$6474 MsgWeaponStored
$649C MsgGenCollected
$64B1 MsgGenDestroyed
$64C6 MsgCongratulations
$654D MsgMalfunction

@module buffers ; The exhaust, the blank graphic, the ED flags and the sprite save buffers
@about The exhaust is a flicker of four three-byte patterns under the droid (DroidExhaust $8DD2); NewGame $7A44 zeroes $65E5-$668B (the exhaust rows, BlankGraphic and EdFlags). The image's bytes from $65E5 are Leftover text (the authors' source, src/res/leftover/Source3.txt): every one is written before it is read.

$65AE w ExhaustPtrs 4 ; The four exhaust rows' pattern records
> ExhaustStep $8E7E steps the four rows through ExhaustRow / ExhaustRowLeft with these (L1 18 571 passes). The image's values point at the first four ExhaustPatterns.

$65B6 b ExhaustPatterns 4 ; Eight exhaust pattern records: a life counter, three bytes of pixels
> ExhaustRow $8EC7 rotates a record's three bytes one pixel a pass; when its counter runs out ExhaustPick $8EAC takes a random record whose counter is 0 and gives it 8-39 passes (Random $7DC9: L1 5 012 tries, 3 134 picks).

$65D6 b ExhaustMasks 2 ; The masks of the exhaust rows: $FF $00 (every frame but 12), $0F $F0 (frame 12)
> ExhaustMask $8F1B ANDs the three bytes of each row with (mask 1, $FF, mask 2): $65D6 for every frame but 12, $65D8 for frame 12 (DroidFly2 $A168, a side frame; the front frame is 10, DroidFront $A0A0).

$65DA b WeaponColours 3 ; The attributes of the weapon items 1-3 (DrawItem $6E65)
> $43, $44, $46: the same attributes ShowWeapon $79D5 gives the HUD icons of weapons 1-3. DrawItem reads WeaponColours - 1 + the weapon number (L1 25 reads).

$65DD b ShotGraphic0 8 ; Weapon 0's shot: one byte, 8 rows (a short dash on the top row)
> FireBasic $8767 puts it in the shot (L1 734 shots); the interrupt XORs it as a 1 x 1 sprite.

$65E5 b ExhaustRows 3 ; The exhaust's 8 rows of 3 bytes as drawn, then the four pattern rows ($65FD)
> ExhaustMask $8F1B writes the masked rows here; DroidExhaust $8DD2 XORs them under the droid twice a pass. Leftover text in the image.
@res leftover/Source3.bin
$65FD ExhaustRaw ; four rows of three bytes the patterns rotate through

$6609 b BlankGraphic 8 ; 120 zero bytes: the frame of an empty or materialising sprite
> NewGame zeroes it; DrawRoom gives it to the waiting enemies ($6BB0), the droid starts with it (InitialVars), ShowWeapon $7A17 prints it to blank the ammunition.
@res leftover/Source3.bin

$6681 b EdFlags 10 ; The ED pieces taken: ten bytes, bits 0-4 of each, 50 pieces
> ProgressScreen $8F53, called by EdPickup $7703 with the piece number, sets the bit of a piece; WinTest $69E3 waits for all ten bytes to be $1F; ProgressScreen $8F53 shows them. NewGame zeroes them.
@res leftover/Source3.bin
$668B EdFlagsEnd

$668C b DroidSave 8 ; The droid's frame as last drawn: 120 bytes
> MoveDroid $72AD copies the frame graphic here each pass, so the interrupt can XOR the old image out with the same bytes it drew (L1 15 289).
@res leftover/Source3.bin

$6704 b EnemyBufferPtrs 4 ; The enemy buffers in use: the draw buffer, the erase buffer
> MainLoop sets both before each MoveEnemy call ($6938-$6977). The image's four bytes are Leftover text ("l", 6, "lookup"), not addresses.
@res leftover/Source3.bin

$6708 b EnemyBuffers 8 ; Four pairs of 48-byte buffers: an enemy's graphic shifted to its pixel x, as drawn and as to erase
> Enemy k uses $6708 + 96k and $6738 + 96k (MainLoop). MoveEnemy shifts the enemy's graphic into one (ShiftGraphic3 $7EBB) and hands both to the sprite list.
@res leftover/Source3.bin
$6738 EnemyBuffer1B
$6768 EnemyBuffer2A
$6798 EnemyBuffer2B
$67C8 EnemyBuffer3A
$67F8 EnemyBuffer3B
$6828 EnemyBuffer4A
$6858 EnemyBuffer4B

@module droidtables ; The droid's frame tables and the animation tables of the sprites
@about The frame tables of the droid, the turn-round, the enemies, the explosions and the weapon-3 shot.
$6888 w DroidFrames 7 ; The droid's 14 frame graphics
> MoveDroid $752D: DroidGraphic = DroidFrames[DroidFrame], DroidWidth = DroidWidths[DroidFrame] (L1 15 293). The graphics are DroidGraphics $9C40-$A257.

$68A4 b DroidWidths 14 ; The width in bytes of each frame
$68B2 b TurnSequence 6 ; The frames of the turn-round: 8, 9, 10, 9, 8, then $FF
> TurnRound $7866 plays it (L1 538 steps).
$68B8 b EnemyFrameCounts 9 ; The frames of each of the nine enemy kinds
> EnemyKind $90C8 reads EnemyFrameCounts - 1 + the kind (1-9): 4, 3, then 4 for kinds 3-9.
$68C1 w EnemyGraphics 9 ; The graphics of the nine enemy kinds ($A74E-$AEC4)
> EnemyKind returns HL = EnemyGraphics - 2 + 2 x kind. Each frame is 49 bytes: the shift byte, then 16 rows of 3 bytes (ShiftGraphic3).
$68D3 w ExplosionFrames 4 ; The four frames of an enemy's explosion
$68DB w MaterialiseFrames 4 ; The four frames of an enemy's materialising
$68E3 w ObjectDeathFrames 4 ; The four frames of an object's death
> The first word (ObjectDeath $B6BA) is never read: the dying states (state AND $0C = $04, $08, $0C) select offsets 2, 4, 6; $00 ends the death first (MoveObjectDying $8236).
$68EB w Weapon3Frames 4 ; The four frames of weapon 3's shot

@module main ; The start, the main loop, the hazard, the win and the life lost
@about Setup puts the stack under the game block, selects the keyboard and installs the IM 2 handler. Restart shows the menu and starts a game. MainLoop is one pass of the game with two HALTs (WaitFrame $795D): the droid and its exhaust between the first and the second, the objects, enemies and patrollers after the second. The interrupt handler $95D0 draws the sprite list at each interrupt, so a pass holds two interrupts when its work fits (and three when the work, run with interrupts off, overruns one).

$68F3 c Setup ; Power-on setup: the stack, the keyboard, the IM 2 handler
> DI; SP = $60C0 (the stack grows down under the cell table, in the BASIC area); Controls = $80 (the keyboard); SetupIM2 $9584. Runs once (c1 L1 1).

$6900 c Restart ; The menu, then a new game
> ClearScreen $7A9F, Menu $8854 (1, 2, 3 choose the controls, 0 starts), NewGame $7A2F, the message TUJAD FROM ORPHEUS, EI, then MainLoop. Entered at the start and after a win ($6A2D) or a game over ($6AAA). L1 4 (c1 and the menu builds).

$6911 c MainLoop ; One pass of the game: two interrupts (three when the work overruns)
> WaitFrame (the sprites of the last half pass are drawn), PassTick $7F6D (a sound tick), DroidExhaust $8DD2 (erase), MoveDroid $7208, ExhaustStep $8E7E, DroidExhaust (draw), HazardFlash $699B, MoveShot $8298; WaitFrame; MoveObject $809D for both objects; MoveEnemy $90E7 for the four enemies, each with its pair of EnemyBuffers; MovePatrollers $6AAD; MoveShot again; Pause $793A (ENTER); EnergyOut $6A30; WinTest $69E3; again.
> Witness: L1 18 585 passes. Most passes hold exactly two interrupts (the two HALTs); a pass whose work between the HALTs runs past the next interrupt misses it (the work runs with interrupts off) and waits for the one after, so it holds three. Counted by the interrupts inside it, a pass holds 2 (183 of 200 from rzx/at050000.szx) or 3 (17: one INT lost while the work ran with interrupts off); never 1. The "1-frame" passes of a count by frame number are passes that start before T 1812, where unreal-ng's 48K INT falls (doc 4.7).
$6929 MainLoopSecondHalf ; the second WaitFrame: the objects, the enemies and the patrollers follow

$6992 c HazardDeath ; A touched hazard: the life ends unless the droid is turning round
> Reached from MoveDroid's touch test ($75E1) when a cell under the droid has a mark other than an enemy's (a patroller or an object: bits 2-6). DroidFlags bit 1 (turning) protects; otherwise LoseLife. L1 53.

$699B c HazardFlash ; While the shield is up: flash the droid's attributes and click the beeper
> Only while DroidFlags bit 4 (the shield, down held alone; the energy drains fast, EnergyReload 10) is set: fills the droid's 5 attribute rows with DeathColour (2-7, cycling) + BRIGHT (OR $40), toggling the beeper bit (port $FE) each row. L1 18 574 calls, 104 flashes.
$69C3 HazardFlashColour
$69CA HazardFlashRow
$69CD HazardFlashCell

$69E3 c WinTest ; Every ED piece taken: EDS COMPLETED; back in room $54: the end
> All ten EdFlags bytes $1F (L1 18 581 tests): the eleventh byte (EdFlagsEnd) is set to $1F once and the message EDS COMPLETED queued. Then in room $54 (the control room, where the game starts): the attributes $5885-$5889 (row 4, columns 5-9) set to 0, which hides the lives and energy digits, CONGRATULATIONS queued, EnergyTimer and ScrollPriority zeroed, a delay (WinDelay, BC = $FDE8), ProgressScreen with A = 0 (the whole picture), 512 HALTs (WinWait: B = 0, C = 2; about 10 s) and Restart. Witness: the RZX log (work/stage1/rzx/frames.bin) queues EDS COMPLETED at RZX frame 71 845 in room 28 (ScrollNext $63E8, priority 10), enters room 84 at 79 916 and runs WinTestRoom at 79 947 (ScrollNext $64C7, priority 20, EnergyTimer 0); the win recording starts inside WinWait ($6A26: 495 HALTs, then Restart, Menu at its frame 497). $69FF-$6A25 ran only in the RZX playback, which no L1 build holds.
$69EA WinTestLoop
$69F9 WinTestRoom
$6A04 WinClearRow
$6A19 WinDelay
$6A26 WinWait

$6A30 c EnergyOut ; Energy 0 and not turning: a life lost
> L1 18 582 tests, 2 with energy 0 (energy-out).

$6A3C c LoseLife ; A life ends: the death effect, the droid placed again, or the game over
> Energy back to 99 and the timers to $FA; Lives - 1 (BCD); GEN 19 DESTROYED; DeathEffect $7ADF; SaveRoomObjects; PlaceDroid $7A6D; the shield down (DroidFlags bit 4 cleared) and the touch test back on (FlagPointer = DroidFlags); then, with lives left, the HUD's lives and energy printed (PrintBcd) and DrawRoom with A = $FF (a clear, no scroll). With Lives = $99 (0 - 1 in BCD): the game over at LoseLifeGameOver. L1 55 lives lost (energy-out 2, game-over).
$6A83 LoseLifeGameOver ; COMPUTER MALFUNCTION, ProgressScreen with A = $FF, a wait, Restart (L1 1: game-over)
$6A88 GameOverClearRow
$6AA3 GameOverWait

$6AAD c MovePatrollers ; Move one of the three patrollers a pass
> PatrollerTurn cycles 0-2; PatrollerPtr = PatrollerPtrs[turn]; MovePatroller $93A6. L1 18 583.
$6AB6 MovePatrollersSet

$6ACD c QueueMessage ; Queue the message at HL when its priority is above the one shown
> In: HL a message (Messages). When (HL) > ScrollPriority: ScrollPriority = (HL), ScrollNext = HL + 1. L1 86 calls, 79 taken.

@module rooms ; The room: its record, its drawing, its items and objects
@about The world is 128 rooms in a grid 8 rooms across and 16 down (room + 1 east, + 8 south... the exits of MoveDroid $7403-$7527 add 1, subtract 1, add 8 and subtract 8). Two tables give each room: the layout (RoomLayouts $BD06: the tiles) and the alien map (AlienMap $DB53: the items, the teleporter, the enemies and the objects). 26 rooms are empty: their layout is $DB52 and their alien record $DFFA. The editable form of both is src/res/rooms/.

$6ADC c SaveRoomObjects ; Write the room's two object records back into its alien record
> Walks the room's record past the item and teleporter bytes (each 1 or 2 bytes, 0 = none) and the enemy mask, and copies Objects / Object2 (10 bytes each, Object1Flags bit 6 set) over the record's object fields when they are present. Called on every exit and teleport and on a life lost: L1 276.
$6AF4 SaveObjectsItem2
$6AF9 SaveObjectsTeleport
$6AFE SaveObjectsMask
$6B12 SaveObjectsNoObject1
$6B13 SaveObjectsObject2

$6B22 c DrawRoom ; Enter the room: reset the movers, read the alien record, wipe and draw the room, build the cells
> In: A = 0 a scroll wipe (an exit), A not 0 a clear (a life lost, a teleport, a new game); Room. The sprite list is emptied; the patrollers marked not drawn; the four enemy records and the two objects zeroed. From the room's alien record (AlienMap + the word at AlienMap + 2 x Room): Item1Pos / Item1Kind, Item2Pos / Item2Kind, TeleportPos / TeleportRoom; the enemy mask (bit k: enemy k waits to spawn, timer 1 or 2, state $40, BlankGraphic); the two object records copied into Objects. The play area's attributes keep BRIGHT and INK (AND $47: paper and FLASH cleared); then the wipe: DrawRoomScroll shifts every line of the play area one pixel out, odd lines left and even lines right, eight times with a delay (L1 171 scrolls), or ClearPlayArea $7917 (110). The attributes are zeroed, then the layout (RoomLayouts + RoomLayouts[2 x Room]) is drawn tile by tile: (column, row, tile) triples until a column byte with bit 7. Each tile's pixels come from TileGraphics[tile] (width, height, rows) and its colours from TileAttrs[tile] (a fill byte with bit 7, or one attribute a cell), all clipped to the play area. Then the cell table (1 where the attribute is not 0), the items (ItemCell $6E88, DrawItem $6E0D for a kind with bit 7) and the teleporter ($FF $FF, TeleportColumn / TeleportRow). Shot = 0; DI.
> Witness: L1 279 rooms drawn; 6 458 tiles; the attributes' loop 161 103 cells.
$6B48 DrawRoomClearEnemies
$6B52 DrawRoomClearObjects
$6B79 DrawRoomItem2
$6B87 DrawRoomTeleport
$6B95 DrawRoomEnemies
$6BBD DrawRoomEnemy2
$6BDE DrawRoomEnemy3
$6BFF DrawRoomEnemy4
$6C20 DrawRoomObjects
$6C30 DrawRoomNoObject1
$6C32 DrawRoomObject2
