@module bitreverse ; The bit-reverse table and the unused zero fills around it
@about A 256-byte table of each byte with its bits reversed, which TurnRound $7866 uses to mirror the droid, between two zero fills that no code reads or writes (L1: no access).
$96FE s ZeroFill1 846 ; 846 zero bytes no code reads or writes
> L1, c1-c4 and the win recording: no read, no write in $96FE-$9A4B. A space the authors left between the code and the tables.
$9A4C b BitReverse 16 ; Each byte with its bits reversed: BitReverse[n] = n with bit 0 as bit 7 ... bit 7 as bit 0
> TurnMirror2 / TurnMirror3 ($7866) look up each graphic byte here (L = byte, H = $9A). Checked: all 256 entries are the exact bit reversal of their index. L1: 137 of the 256 entries read, 180 464 reads.
$9B4C s ZeroFill2 244 ; 244 zero bytes no code reads or writes
> L1: no read, no write in $9B4C-$9C3F.

@module sprites ; The sprite graphics: the droid, the enemies, the weapon-3 probe, the objects, the patrollers, the menu frame
@about The graphics of every moving thing. The droid's frames are rows of 2 or 3 bytes, 40 rows; the enemy, materialise and probe frames 16 rows of 3 bytes after a shift byte; the object frames 24 rows of 4 bytes after a shift byte; the patroller frames 24 rows of 4 bytes. ShiftGraphic3 / ShiftGraphic4 shift the enemy and object frames in place by the shift byte, so the bytes of $A74E-$AEC4, $B049-$B2EF, $B413-$B6B9 and $B71B-$B83D change in play (L1 writes); every shift byte in the image is 0 (the frames as drawn).
$9C40 b DroidGraphics 24 ; The droid's 14 frames, 40 rows each: frames 0-7 and 11-13 3 bytes wide, frames 8-10 (the turn and the front) 2 bytes
> DroidFrames $6888 points at each frame, DroidWidths $68A4 gives its width: 0-7 the walk (3 bytes, 120 each), 8 and 9 the turn-round (2 bytes), 10 the front, also the teleport beam (2 bytes, DroidFront), 11-13 the droid in the air (3 bytes; 13 never stays on screen). TurnRound $7866 mirrors $9C40-$A4AF in place (480 + 480 + 160 + 480 + 480 = 2 080 bytes), all but DroidFront, so the image holds the right-facing set and play turns it; PlaceDroid $7A6D reads DroidGraphics + 4 ($70 in the right-facing set) to know which way the frames face. L1: all 1 560 bytes read (3 586 417 reads), 1 480 written (127 784 writes: the mirroring; DroidFront's 80 bytes never).
$9CB8 DroidWalk1
$9D30 DroidWalk2
$9DA8 DroidWalk3
$9E20 DroidWalk4
$9E98 DroidWalk5
$9F10 DroidWalk6
$9F88 DroidWalk7
$A000 DroidTurn1
$A050 DroidTurn2
$A0A0 DroidFront
$A0F0 DroidFly1
$A168 DroidFly2
$A1E0 DroidFly3
@res sprites/Droid.bin
$A258 s DroidMirrorSpare 600 ; 600 zero bytes inside the mirrored range
> TurnRound's last 3-byte pass runs to $A4AF (from $A2D0, 160 rows of 3 bytes), so it mirrors these zeros too (L1: 600 bytes, 52 680 reads and as many writes, all zeros); no frame points here.
$A4B0 s ZeroFill3 670 ; 670 zero bytes no code reads or writes
> L1: no access in $A4B0-$A74D.
$A74E b EnemyGraphicsData 49 ; The nine enemy kinds: 35 frames of 49 bytes (shift byte, 16 rows of 3 bytes)
> EnemyGraphics $68C1 points at each kind's first frame, EnemyFrameCounts $68B8 gives its frames (4, 3, then 4). ShiftGraphic3 shifts a frame in place to the enemy's x. L1: every byte of $A74E-$AEC4 read (1 244 984 reads) and written (375 902 writes).
$A812 EnemyKind2
$A8A5 EnemyKind3
$A969 EnemyKind4
$AA2D EnemyKind5
$AAF1 EnemyKind6
$ABB5 EnemyKind7
$AC79 EnemyKind8
$AD3D EnemyKind9
$AE01 EnemyExplosion ; the four frames of an enemy's explosion (ExplosionFrames $68D3)
$AE32 EnemyExplosion2
$AE63 EnemyExplosion3
$AE94 EnemyExplosion4
@res sprites/Enemies.bin
$AEC5 b MaterialiseGraphics 48 ; An enemy materialising: 4 frames of 48 bytes (16 rows of 3, no shift byte)
> MaterialiseFrames $68DB. The frames are drawn at a byte column, so they are not shifted. L1: 192 bytes, 178 998 reads, no write.
$AEF5 Materialise2
$AF25 Materialise3
$AF55 Materialise4
@res sprites/Materialise.bin
$AF85 b ProbeGraphics 49 ; Weapon 3's probe: 4 frames of 49 bytes (shift byte, 16 rows of 3)
> FireWeapon3 ($861C) and MoveShot3Frame ($8298 on) point the shot at frame (ShotFrame AND 3): 0, 1, 2, 3, then back down. L1: only the recordings rzx-040000, rzx-052000 and rzx-070000 fire weapon 3; they read frames 2 and 3 ($AFB6-$B017, 291 reads), so frames 1 and 4 are known only from the code there. The recording probe-shield (tjB, doc 05 5.8) shows frames 2 and 4 too (rows at $AFB7 and $B019, counting from 1 as above), so only frame 1 is known only from the code. No write: the shift was 0 each time.
$AFB6 Probe2
$AFE7 Probe3
$B018 Probe4
@res sprites/Probe.bin
$B049 b ObjectGraphics 97 ; The five object kinds: 17 frames of 97 bytes (shift byte, 24 rows of 4 bytes)
> ObjectKind $7C28 by the kind bits: bit 4 $B049 (3 frames), bit 5 $B16C (4), bit 6 $B2F0 (3), bit 7 $B413 (4), none $B597 (3). ShiftGraphic4 shifts a frame in place to the object's x. L1: all 1 649 bytes read (355 694 reads), 1 358 written (69 341 writes: $B049-$B2EF and $B413-$B6B9; kind 6 at $B2F0 never shifted).
$B16C ObjectKind5
$B2F0 ObjectKind6
$B413 ObjectKind7
$B597 ObjectKindNone
$B6BA ObjectDeath ; the four frames of an object's death (ObjectDeathFrames $68E3); L1 reads frames 2-4 only (8 679 reads): frame 1 is never read (MoveObjectDying)
$B71B ObjectDeath2
$B77C ObjectDeath3
$B7DD ObjectDeath4
$B83E ProbeExplosion ; the four frames of the probe's explosion (Weapon3Frames $68EB), 49 bytes each; L1 1 164 reads, all in the three weapon-3 recordings
$B86F ProbeExplosion2
$B8A0 ProbeExplosion3
$B8D1 ProbeExplosion4
@res sprites/Objects.bin
$B902 b PatrolRight 32 ; A patroller going right: 4 frames of 96 bytes (24 rows of 4)
> DrawPatroller $942D (at $9430) takes this set for a patroller moving right (direction bit 0 of +1 clear) and PatrolLeft for one moving left. Not shifted: a patroller moves a byte column at a time. L1: 768 bytes, 96 192 reads, no write.
$B962 PatrolRight2
$B9C2 PatrolRight3
$BA22 PatrolRight4
$BA82 PatrolLeft ; a patroller going left
$BAE2 PatrolLeft2
$BB42 PatrolLeft3
$BBA2 PatrolLeft4
@res sprites/Patrollers.bin
$BC02 b MenuFrameTopEdge 32 ; The menu screen's frame: 8 blocks of 16 x 16 pixels (16 rows of 2 bytes)
> Menu $8854 draws the frame with Blit16: the top edge 14 times along row 0, the bottom edge along row 22, the left and right edges 10 times down columns 0 and 30, and the four corners. L1 reads 4 992 (4 menus x 1 248).
$BC22 MenuFrameBottomEdge
$BC42 MenuFrameLeftEdge
$BC62 MenuFrameRightEdge
$BC82 MenuFrameTopLeft
$BCA2 MenuFrameTopRight
$BCC2 MenuFrameBottomLeft
$BCE2 MenuFrameBottomRight
@res graphics/MenuFrame.bin
$BD02 s ZeroFill4 4 ; 4 zero bytes no code reads

@module roomdata ; The world: the room layouts and the alien map
@about Two tables of 128 rooms (8 across, 16 down: room + 1 is east, room + 8 south). Each starts with 128 words, the offset of the room's record from the table's start, then the records in room order. 102 rooms have records; the other 26 point at the empty layout $DB52 and the empty alien record $DFFA. The editable form is src/res/rooms/Rooms.txt (one section per room).
$BD06 w RoomLayouts 8 ; The layout offsets: room n's tiles at RoomLayouts + RoomLayouts[n]
> DrawRoomLayout $6C93 reads it (L1 285 rooms drawn; 224 of the 256 bytes read, 560 reads). The offsets of the 102 full rooms rise in room order: the records are contiguous.
@res rooms/Rooms.bin
$BE06 b RoomLayoutRecords 3 ; The layouts: (column, row, tile) triples, ended by a byte with bit 7 set ($80)
> DrawRoomLayout $6C93 draws tile n (TileGraphics $6EA8, TileAttrs $6F36) with its top-left character at column, row (row from the top of the play area). L1: 7 343 of the 7 501 bytes read (19 362 reads; the rest are rooms no recording entered). The tiles of a room also make its cells (CellTable $60C0): bit 0 of a cell marks a wall.
@res rooms/Rooms.bin
$DB52 EmptyLayout ; the layout of the 26 empty rooms: no tile
$DB53 w AlienMap 8 ; The alien map offsets: room n's record at AlienMap + the word at AlienMap + 2n
> RoomRecord $7B8F (L1 4 746 calls), DrawRoom (279), SaveRoomObjects $6ADC (276), the item pickup (50). L1: all 256 bytes read, 9 184 reads.
@res rooms/Rooms.bin
$DC53 b AlienRecords 8 ; The alien records: items, teleporter, enemies and objects of each room
> A record: item 1's position byte (0: none; else its kind follows), item 2's position (0 or position and kind), the teleporter's position (0 or position and target room), the enemy mask (bit k: enemy k may spawn here), object 1 (0, or its 10-byte record as Objects holds it), object 2 (the same). A position byte is (column / 2) x 16 + the row in cells (ItemCell $6E88). The records change in play: ScrambleRooms $7BA0 moves the item kinds, SaveRoomObjects writes the objects back, a pickup zeroes a position; the image holds the start of a game as the authors saved it. L1: 937 of 941 bytes read (17 272 reads), 326 written (3 155 writes).
$DFFA EmptyAlienRecord ; the record of the empty rooms: six zeros
@res rooms/Rooms.bin
$E000 b FontPad 2 ; 2 zero bytes before the font; the scroller reads $E001 as line 0 of a space
> Interrupt $95D0 takes Font - 257 + 8 x code + ScrollLine, so line 0 of a character is the last line of the one before it; for a space that is $E001 (L1 772 reads). $E000 is unread.
$E002 b Font 8 ; The font: 60 characters of 8 bytes, codes $20-$5B (space to '[')
> PrintText $8AB4 (LD BC,Font - 256: Font - $100 + 8 x code), the scroller (Font - 257 + 8 x code + the line). L1: 282 of the 480 bytes read (25 630 reads); the characters no text uses stay unread.
@res graphics/Font.bin
$E1E2 b EdPieces 32 ; The picture the ED pieces make: 50 tiles of 16 x 16 pixels (16 rows of 2 bytes), 10 x 5
> Tile row x 10 + column. Piece n (kind $81 + n) is the tile at column (n - 1) / 5, row (n - 1) % 5, so EdFlags byte (n - 1) / 5 is a column of the picture and its bit (n - 1) % 5 the row. DrawItem $6E0D draws a piece lying in a room (LD HL,EdPieces - 352 at $6E34, then 32 a column and 320 a row; worked out for n = 1-50); ProgressScreen $8F53 draws the found tiles at $4886. L1: all 1 600 bytes read, 28 384 reads.
@res graphics/EdPieces.bin
$E822 b TileData 16 ; The tile graphics: blocks of [width in bytes][height in pixel rows][width x height bytes, top row first]
> TileGraphics $6EA8 points at each tile's block; DrawRoomLayout draws them (DrawTilePixels $6CE8: the rows go down the screen, a byte column at a time; bytes over the HUD rows are skipped). The 70 blocks the table points at do not overlap and do not follow the tile order. Two parts no code reads: the 56 zero bytes $F8A6-$F8DD between blocks, and the block of the 71st table word, $FDDC-$FDFD (2 x 16, a graphic no room uses: the word itself is never read), then 2 bytes $FDFE-$FDFF. L1: 4 728 of the 5 598 bytes read (479 258 reads); $FDDC-$FDFF: no access.
@res tiles/TileData.bin
